{
  "id": "fluency-l10-undo-product",
  "lesson": "l10-undo-product",
  "title": "Fluency: integration by parts",
  "n": 20,
  "pass": 0.9,
  "minutes": 14,
  "families": [
    {
      "id": "parts-exp",
      "weight": 1,
      "skill": "integrate a power times an exponential",
      "vars": {
        "a": { "int": [-9, 9], "not": [0] },
        "k": { "int": [-3, 3], "not": [0] },
        "v": { "pick": ["x", "t"] }
      },
      "let": { "r": "a/k", "s": "a/(k*k)" },
      "where": ["r != 1 and r != -1", "s != 1 and s != -1"],
      "prompt": "Find $\\int {a:c}{v}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}\\,d{v}$.",
      "widget": { "kind": "expression", "answer": "{r:frac}{v}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}{-s:+frac}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}", "upToConstant": true, "keyboard": "calculus" },
      "wrong": [
        { "id": "sign-slip", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}{s:+frac}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}", "line": "Parts is $uv - \\int v\\,du$: subtract the second term, do not add it." },
        { "id": "missing-term", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}", "line": "You stopped at $uv$. Subtract $\\int v\\,du$ as well." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        "Take $u = {a:c}{v}$, which differentiating makes a constant, and $dv = e^{{{k:c}{v}}}\\,d{v}$.",
        "$uv = {r:frac}{v}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}$ and $\\int v\\,du = {s:frac}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}$.",
        "So the answer is $uv - \\int v\\,du = {r:frac}{v}e^{{{k:c}{v}}}{-s:+frac}e^{{{k:c}{v}}} + C$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "parts-cos",
      "weight": 1,
      "skill": "integrate a power times a cosine",
      "vars": {
        "a": { "int": [-9, 9], "not": [0] },
        "k": { "int": [1, 4] },
        "v": { "pick": ["x", "t"] }
      },
      "let": { "r": "a/k", "s": "a/(k*k)" },
      "where": ["r != 1 and r != -1", "s != 1 and s != -1"],
      "prompt": "Find $\\int {a:c}{v}\\cos({k:c}{v})\\,d{v}$.",
      "widget": { "kind": "expression", "answer": "{r:frac}{v}\\sin({k:c}{v}){s:+frac}\\cos({k:c}{v})", "upToConstant": true, "keyboard": "calculus" },
      "wrong": [
        { "id": "sign-slip", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}\\sin({k:c}{v}){-s:+frac}\\cos({k:c}{v})", "line": "$\\int v\\,du$ holds a sine, and the integral of a sine is minus a cosine: the two minus signs make a plus." },
        { "id": "missing-term", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}\\sin({k:c}{v})", "line": "You stopped at $uv$. Subtract $\\int v\\,du$ as well." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        "Take $u = {a:c}{v}$ and $dv = \\cos({k:c}{v})\\,d{v}$.",
        "$uv = {r:frac}{v}\\sin({k:c}{v})$ and $\\int v\\,du = {-s:frac}\\cos({k:c}{v})$.",
        "So the answer is $uv - \\int v\\,du = {r:frac}{v}\\sin({k:c}{v}){s:+frac}\\cos({k:c}{v}) + C$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "parts-sin",
      "weight": 1,
      "skill": "integrate a power times a sine",
      "vars": {
        "a": { "int": [-9, 9], "not": [0] },
        "k": { "int": [1, 4] },
        "v": { "pick": ["x", "t"] }
      },
      "let": { "r": "a/k", "s": "a/(k*k)" },
      "where": ["r != 1 and r != -1", "s != 1 and s != -1"],
      "prompt": "Find $\\int {a:c}{v}\\sin({k:c}{v})\\,d{v}$.",
      "widget": { "kind": "expression", "answer": "{-r:frac}{v}\\cos({k:c}{v}){s:+frac}\\sin({k:c}{v})", "upToConstant": true, "keyboard": "calculus" },
      "wrong": [
        { "id": "sign-slip", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}\\cos({k:c}{v}){s:+frac}\\sin({k:c}{v})", "line": "The integral of a sine is minus a cosine, so $uv$ carries a minus sign." },
        { "id": "missing-term", "tex": "{-r:frac}{v}\\cos({k:c}{v})", "line": "You stopped at $uv$. Subtract $\\int v\\,du$ as well." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        "Take $u = {a:c}{v}$ and $dv = \\sin({k:c}{v})\\,d{v}$.",
        "$uv = {-r:frac}{v}\\cos({k:c}{v})$ and $\\int v\\,du = {-s:frac}\\sin({k:c}{v})$.",
        "So the answer is $uv - \\int v\\,du = {-r:frac}{v}\\cos({k:c}{v}){s:+frac}\\sin({k:c}{v}) + C$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "parts-log",
      "weight": 1,
      "skill": "integrate a power times a logarithm",
      "vars": {
        "a": { "int": [-9, 9], "not": [0] },
        "n": { "int": [2, 4] },
        "v": { "pick": ["x", "t"] }
      },
      "let": { "n1": "n + 1", "r": "a/(n + 1)", "s": "a/(n + 1)^2" },
      "where": ["r != 1 and r != -1", "s != 1 and s != -1"],
      "prompt": "Find $\\int {a:c}{v}^{{{n}}}\\ln {v}\\,d{v}$ for ${v} > 0$.",
      "widget": { "kind": "expression", "answer": "{r:frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}\\ln({v}){-s:+frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}", "upToConstant": true, "keyboard": "calculus" },
      "wrong": [
        { "id": "sign-slip", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}\\ln({v}){s:+frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}", "line": "Parts is $uv - \\int v\\,du$: subtract the second term, do not add it." },
        { "id": "second-divide", "tex": "{r:frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}\\ln({v}){-r:+frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}", "line": "$\\int v\\,du = \\int {r:frac}{v}^{{{n}}}\\,d{v}$: raise the power to {n1} and divide by {n1} again." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        "Take $u = \\ln {v}$, which differentiating makes simpler, and $dv = {a:c}{v}^{{{n}}}\\,d{v}$, so $v = {r:frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}$.",
        "$\\int v\\,du = \\int {r:frac}{v}^{{{n}}}\\,d{v} = {s:frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}$.",
        "So the answer is ${r:frac}{v}^{{{n1}}}\\ln {v}{-s:+frac}{v}^{{{n1}}} + C$."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
